# Figma Feature Files

This project's feature-file tool. Owns the per-feature workspaces where design work happens, inside team **{{TEAM}}** / project **{{PROJECT}}**.

## Built on figma-use

This skill is an **overlay** on [figma-use](../figma-use/SKILL.md), Figma's official skill for driving the MCP server. `figma-use` owns the plugin-API mechanics. This skill owns the project's conventions — naming, the minimal page structure, the handoff signal.

**Whenever an operation here requires programmatic execution in Figma, follow `figma-use` for how to drive the API.** This skill never duplicates that material.

## Owned operations

- **create feature file** — set up a new file with the minimal page structure below.
- **open feature file** — locate the existing file for a feature or epic.
- **comment on feature file** — add Figma comments or sticky annotations.
- **mark frame ready for development** — move approved frames to the Ready for Development page and apply the Dev Mode status.
- **archive frame** — move rejected explorations to the Archive page with a brief note.

## Access

All operations go through the **Figma MCP server**, driven via `figma-use`. Direct REST API calls or manual edits outside the MCP server are not permitted.

### Preflight (first operation of the session)

1. **Load figma-use.** Read [figma-use/SKILL.md](../figma-use/SKILL.md) before any `use_figma` call.
2. **Server health.** Verify the Figma MCP server is reachable and authenticated.
3. **Team / project access.** Verify the server can access team **{{TEAM}}** and project **{{PROJECT}}**.

## File organisation

One feature file per feature or epic. Sits alongside the Design System file inside project **{{PROJECT}}**.

### File naming

`[Feature/Epic Name]` — e.g., `Onboarding`, `Payment Flow`, `Settings Redesign`. No generic names like `Untitled`.

## File structure

The minimal page structure — three pages, that's it:

- **Cover** — feature name, status (Draft / In Review / Approved / In Development / Complete), owner, link to the corresponding work item or milestone (when one exists).
- **Design** — where the work lives. Designers organise this however the work calls for: flows, wireframes, high-fi mocks, prototypes — at whatever fidelity each part of the work needs. Use Figma Sections to group related frames if the file gets large. No prescribed sub-page structure.
- **Ready for Development** — the signed-off, final frames developers reference. Approved by the user/stakeholder, marked with Figma's Dev Mode "Ready for development" status. The definitive source of truth for implementation.

An optional **Archive / Exploration** page can hold rejected options with brief notes on why — useful for big features, unnecessary for small ones.

That's the whole structure. Add more pages if the work genuinely needs them; otherwise three is enough.

## Conventions

### Frame and layer naming

- **Frames** are named descriptively — what they represent, not `Frame 42`. Sequential prefixes (`01_`, `02_`) help when a flow has a natural order.
- **Layers** describe their content or role (`Header`, `CTA Button`, `Error Message`).
- **Sections** group related frames with clear titles.

### Visual production

When producing high-fidelity designs:

- Use components and tokens from the published design system (no detached instances, no hardcoded values).
- Cover the interactive states relevant to the feature.

When the design system needs an addition, round-trip through `figma-design-system` — don't extend ad-hoc inside a feature file.

### Handoff

- Approved frames move to the **Ready for Development** page and carry Figma's Dev Mode "Ready for development" status.
- Layer names, component properties, and variables are clean and meaningful — Dev Mode is the developer's interface to the design.
- Annotations (Figma comments or sticky notes) explain interaction intent, state transitions, and accessibility requirements that aren't self-evident from the visual.

## Composition

When the `figma-design-system` skill is installed, feature files consume its published library. Token or component changes round-trip through `figma-design-system`, then propagate here on republish.
